# porthole -- pacman install scriptlet for the base package.
#
# Three functions. `pre_remove` is the one that had to exist: removal has to
# close what porthole opened, because nothing else will. `post_upgrade` is
# the same hazard in its other form: an upgrade has to end the helper the
# previous version left running, because nothing else will either.
# `post_install` only says something -- that the notification agent is not
# running yet -- and says it once.
#
# Every other way a porthole rule ends needs porthole to still be installed:
# the expiry timer re-executes /usr/bin/porthole, `close` and the
# network-change monitor go through the helper, and reconciliation runs
# inside an operation. Removing the package ends all of them at once and
# leaves the firewall rule enforced, with the state file that names it on a
# tmpfs and nothing left that can act on it.
#
# Why a scriptlet at all, given the PKGBUILD's own note that this package
# needs none: install scripts are uncommon here -- 10 of the 137 packages in
# the archlinux:latest base image ship one, and 2 of those 10 define a removal
# hook (p11-kit disables its systemd units in pre_remove; systemd edits
# etc/shells in post_remove) -- and the *post-install* work this layout needs
# really is covered by pacman hooks other packages already ship. This is not
# that work. A pacman hook could have carried it (`Operation = Remove`,
# `When = PreTransaction`, `Target = usr/bin/porthole` fires on exactly this
# package's removal, before its files go); pre_remove was taken instead
# because it ships no installed file to do it, and because the distinction
# that has to be made -- an upgrade must close nothing, or it takes away the
# access the user arranged as the price of a version bump -- is then the name
# of the function rather than a Trigger clause. There is no pre_upgrade here,
# so an upgrade closes nothing -- and neither does the post_upgrade added
# below: a `try-restart` ends the helper process, while every rule it opened
# stays in the firewall and in the state file, which
# `RuntimeDirectoryPreserve=yes` keeps across the restart.

post_install() {
	# Say, once, that notifications do not work yet.
	#
	# porthole ships two ways to start the agent -- a systemd user unit and
	# an XDG autostart entry -- and neither of them runs in a session that is
	# already open: `WantedBy=graphical-session.target` is reached when a
	# session begins, and so is /etc/xdg/autostart. This package enables
	# neither, and enabling would not change it: enablement is a symlink, and
	# an install running as root has no user session to start a user unit in.
	#
	# That matters more here than it would for most session services. An
	# announced close is the only signal a timed port has gone -- the window
	# that opened it is usually shut by then -- so a port opened in the
	# session that installed porthole closes with nothing said.
	#
	# post_install and not post_upgrade, deliberately: an upgrade does not
	# change which session the user is in, and a notice repeated on every
	# version bump is one nobody reads on the day it matters.
	# packaging/rpm/porthole.spec and debian/porthole.postinst say the same
	# thing at the same moment.
	cat >&2 <<-NOTICE
		porthole: Desktop notifications start at your next login.
		porthole: A port porthole closes by itself -- on expiry, or on a network
		porthole: change -- is announced only while porthole-agent is running, and
		porthole: neither of its start files runs in a session that is already
		porthole: open. For the session you are in, without logging out:
		porthole:     systemctl --user start porthole-agent.service
	NOTICE
}

post_upgrade() {
	# End the helper the previous version left running.
	#
	# porthole-helper retires itself -- with no rule open and nothing in
	# flight it gives up the bus name and exits after five minutes
	# (crates/porthole-helper/src/retire.rs) -- so on the ordinary machine
	# an upgrade has no process to end and the next call starts the new
	# binary. What is left is the machine where somebody is using porthole
	# at that moment: an open port pins the helper for as long as it stays
	# open, and nothing else on this distribution ends it. pre_remove does not run on an
	# upgrade -- that is the whole point of its name -- and neither systemd
	# pacman hook reaches the process: 30-systemd-daemon-reload-system
	# reloads the manager, which restarts nothing, and
	# 35-systemd-enqueue-marked acts only on units something has marked
	# `needs-restart`, which on Arch nothing does. Measured: with this
	# function absent both hooks ran ("Reloading system manager
	# configuration", "Enqueuing marked services") and the helper's PID was
	# the same before and after, its /proc/PID/exe reading
	# `/usr/libexec/porthole-helper (deleted)`. That is the same hazard
	# pre_remove's last line was written for, in its upgrade form.
	#
	# The failure that makes it worth a scriptlet is that it is silent.
	# porthole's D-Bus wire format has already changed incompatibly once:
	# adding three members to WireRule moved the RuleClosed signal from
	# ((sqssssttu)s) to ((sqssssttusqq)s), and zbus drops a signal whose
	# signature does not match instead of raising anything. A stale helper
	# does not fail against a new agent, it goes quiet -- the user stops
	# being told their port closed and has no reason to suspect the upgrade.
	#
	# `try-restart` rather than `stop`: both end the stale process, and
	# D-Bus activation would start the new binary on the next call either
	# way, but try-restart is a no-op when the unit is not active, so an
	# upgrade on a machine where nobody has opened a port acquires no root
	# daemon -- and where the helper *was* running, it comes back on the new
	# binary now rather than at the next client call. That window is not
	# nothing: the network-change monitor runs inside the helper process, so
	# a subnet change while it is down goes unnoticed. (An opened rule's
	# automatic close is a transient systemd timer outside the process and
	# survives regardless.) packaging/rpm/porthole.spec's
	# %systemd_postun_with_restart and debian/rules' `--no-start
	# --restart-after-upgrade` are the same decision on the other two
	# distributions, each in its own terms: Debian's ends as a postinst
	# `deb-systemd-invoke try-restart`, while that RPM macro on current
	# systemd-rpm-macros runs no restart itself -- it marks the unit
	# `needs-restart` for systemd's own rpm trigger to act on at the end of
	# the transaction.
	#
	# daemon-reload first, and here rather than left to pacman: the systemd
	# hook that reloads the manager is a PostTransaction hook, so it runs
	# after this function, and a try-restart before it would start the new
	# binary under the unit definition the *previous* version installed.
	#
	# No ordering hazard of pre_remove's kind. That one has to stop the
	# helper after `porthole close --all` because stopping first would let
	# the close D-Bus-activate it again; nothing here calls porthole at all.
	#
	# Arch does have a more idiomatic spelling of these two lines, and it is
	# not taken: /usr/share/libalpm/scripts/systemd-hook carries a `restart`
	# operation ("For use by other packages", `systemctl try-restart "$@"`)
	# meant to be reached from a package's own PostTransaction pacman hook,
	# after the daemon-reload one. Using it means shipping an installed file,
	# and the install layout is the Makefile's -- checked path by path by
	# crates/porthole-cli/tests/install_layout.rs on every `cargo test` --
	# so a file only pacman would ever read has no place in it. Same reason
	# the PKGBUILD gives for pre_remove being a scriptlet and not a hook.
	#
	# Relative paths and the run/systemd/system test, for pre_remove's
	# reasons: pacman runs a scriptlet with the install root as its working
	# directory, and with no systemd running there is no unit to restart.
	[[ -d run/systemd/system ]] || return 0

	systemctl daemon-reload >/dev/null 2>&1 || true
	systemctl try-restart porthole-helper.service >/dev/null 2>&1 || true
	return 0
}

pre_remove() {
	# Relative paths: pacman runs an install scriptlet with the install root
	# as its working directory, so `usr/bin/porthole` is this package's own
	# binary. The systemd and dbus-broker scriptlets in the base image are
	# written the same way.
	#
	# run/systemd/system is the "booted with systemd" test. porthole's
	# helper is a systemd unit and its automatic closes are transient
	# systemd timers; with no systemd running there is nothing this could
	# have opened.
	[[ -d run/systemd/system ]] || return 0
	[[ -x usr/bin/porthole ]] || return 0

	# `porthole close --all` rather than any shell that guesses at rules:
	# the helper owns the firewall and holds the record of what it opened,
	# and it also cancels each opening's transient expiry timer as it closes
	# -- a timer that would otherwise fire, after this removal, into a
	# /usr/bin/porthole that is no longer there.
	#
	# Never fatal. An un-removable package is a worse failure than a port
	# left open, and this call can fail for reasons that have nothing to do
	# with the firewall: no system bus (a container, an image build), polkit
	# not running, a helper left broken by an earlier failed upgrade. On any
	# of those the removal continues and the warning below is what the user
	# gets. Closing is not authenticated -- the polkit action
	# com.jacopobriccola.Porthole.close is `yes` for allow_any, allow_active
	# and allow_inactive -- so there is nothing here for a non-interactive
	# removal to be prompted by.
	#
	# The timeout is a bound on how long a removal can sit here: the call
	# reaches a root D-Bus service that drives the firewall, and a bus that
	# never answers must not hang pacman indefinitely.
	echo 'porthole: closing every port porthole still has open, before removing it.' >&2
	local rc=0
	timeout 60 usr/bin/porthole close --all >&2 || rc=$?
	if (( rc != 0 )); then
		cat >&2 <<-WARNING
			porthole: WARNING: \`porthole close --all\` failed (exit $rc).
			porthole: Any port porthole had open is still open, and removing this
			porthole: package leaves nothing behind that would close it. Check with
			porthole: \`firewall-cmd --list-rich-rules\`, \`ufw status numbered\` or
			porthole: \`nft list ruleset\` and remove what is left by hand.
		WARNING
	fi

	# After the close, not before: stopping first would leave the close to
	# D-Bus-activate the helper again. Nothing else reaches this unit on a
	# removal -- post_upgrade above does not run on one, and the systemd
	# pacman hook only reloads the manager -- so without this line removal
	# leaves a root daemon running from a deleted binary, still holding
	# com.jacopobriccola.Porthole.
	systemctl stop porthole-helper.service >/dev/null 2>&1 || true
	return 0
}

# vim:set ft=sh ts=4 sw=4 noet:
